DuckDB build configuration
==========================

WITH_DUCKDB_STORAGE_ENGINE controls the embedded DuckDB engine and its server
integration. It defaults to ON for Linux server builds and OFF on other
platforms. Enabling it explicitly on an unsupported server platform fails during
CMake configuration with an explanation of how to disable it.

  cmake -S . -B build -DWITH_DUCKDB_STORAGE_ENGINE=ON
  cmake -S . -B build-native -DWITH_DUCKDB_STORAGE_ENGINE=OFF

An ON build embeds the engine statically. Its runtime duckdb_mode setting still
defaults to NONE; use duckdb_mode=ON to enable DuckDB table operations. The
administrative CALL dbms_duckdb.query(sql) interface is registered in ON builds.

An OFF build does not configure or build the third-party DuckDB project, link
its static archive, build a DuckDB plugin, or register its native procedure and
system variables. MySQL-side metadata and conversion helpers have no dependency
on DuckDB headers or libraries. OFF does not mean building a loadable module.

A clients-only build (WITHOUT_SERVER=ON) does not enable DuckDB, including when
WITH_DUCKDB_STORAGE_ENGINE is set to ON. DuckDB include directories are supplied
through server target dependencies rather than global include directories.

An OFF server binary cannot open or recover DuckDB tables. Use it with data
directories that do not depend on the DuckDB engine.

Transaction errors
==================

DuckDB does not implement statement-level undo. When the server rolls back a
statement involving DuckDB inside a multi-statement transaction, it rolls back
the complete transaction, including earlier statements, pending DuckDB batches,
other transactional engines and the transaction's binlog cache. The original
statement error is returned. Applications must retry the complete transaction.

  duckdb.statement_rollback
    Covers explicit transactions, autocommit disabled, autocommit errors and
    connection reuse with batching and binary logging both enabled and disabled.

The embedded DuckDB backend does not support savepoints. Once DuckDB has joined
a transaction, SAVEPOINT returns ER_CHECK_NOT_IMPLEMENTED. It does not create a
savepoint that silently ignores later ROLLBACK TO SAVEPOINT operations.

A savepoint created before the transaction first uses DuckDB remains valid:
MySQL rolls back all DuckDB work when rolling back to that earlier savepoint,
and applies the existing savepoint normally to engines already participating.

  duckdb.savepoint_unsupported
    Checks explicit rejection, rollback to an earlier savepoint and native
    InnoDB savepoints with DuckDB batching enabled and disabled.

ALTER TABLE and implicit date defaults
======================================

Adding a DATE or DATETIME NOT NULL column without DEFAULT uses ALGORITHM=COPY.
The SQL layer checks whether existing rows may receive implicit zero dates:
non-strict mode permits them, while strict mode with NO_ZERO_DATE rejects the
operation on a non-empty table. Empty tables can be altered in either mode.
Explicit INSTANT or INPLACE requests for these operations report an unsupported
algorithm error. Other eligible column operations can still use INSTANT.

  duckdb.alter_temporal_default
    Covers the original crash, strict-mode rejection, empty tables, explicit
    algorithms and default-value parity with InnoDB.

Initialization and regression coverage
======================================

DuckDB initialization is serialized by the manager mutex. The database object
is published only after timezone mappings and MySQL UDF registration succeed.
A failed initialization destroys its local database object before another
connection can retry initialization.

  duckdb.concurrent_initialization
    Coordinates two first-use connections with Debug Sync. Both must observe
    the registered MySQL JSON UDF after initialization completes.

  main.duckdb_build_disabled
    Runs in OFF builds. Checks the absent engine, variables and native
    procedure, then exercises native InnoDB transactions and prepared queries.

  duckdb.variables_catalog
    Checks the exact public variable names supplied by the optional engine.
    Generic help and variable-coverage tests exclude these optional entries;
    the persisted-variable test keeps separate strict ON/OFF counts.
